# Vidzeo Muse worker configuration.
# Copy to .env (or set the variables in your shell). Never commit .env.
#
# The worker stores its own bearer token in .muse-agent.json (gitignored).
# No provider or OpenRouter key belongs in this repository. Agent-driven jobs
# are written and rendered by the Muse environment itself.
#
# MUSE INFERENCE IS COMPULSORY. Every vision call, script-writing call and
# Vidnets brain / LLM call must go through Muse via the external_agent
# agent-bridge inbox, answered by the vidnets-inbox-watch hook operator.
# - MUSE_INFERENCE_COMPULSORY=1 makes ScriptIT refuse to run with an
#   OpenRouter key present, and makes the worker refuse to start or render.
# - `node muse-agent.js start` refuses unless the bridge is verified:
#   Vidnets server healthy, agent-bridge inbox writable, vidnets-inbox-watch
#   hook enabled, no OpenRouter key in the environment. MUSE_AUTO_LOOP=1 is
#   a claim mode only — it never exempts the inference check.
# - `node muse-agent.js doctor` checks the same bridge and FAILS when it is
#   absent. A job with no Muse-answered vision verdicts is failed, never
#   delivered as graphics.
MUSE_INFERENCE_COMPULSORY=1

# CMS address. Pairing writes the chosen address into .muse-agent.json.
MUSE_CMS_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3210

# Optional worker log verbosity: info | debug
MUSE_LOG_LEVEL=info

# --- Narration (ScriptIT) ---------------------------------------------------
# Default engine: the ScriptIT headless CLI (scriptit/cli/generate.js). It
# researches the title, writes narration through the editorial gate, and
# needs no OpenRouter key — Muse answers its inference requests through the
# Vidnets agent-bridge inbox (see scriptit/CLI.md). No setting is needed.
# Agent-bridge mode gives each inference request a 45-minute budget because
# the operator answers at human speed; override with:
# MUSE_SCRIPT_TIMEOUT_MS=2700000
#
# Inbox answer window: the Vidnets server floors every agent-bridge request
# at VIDZEO_AGENT_MIN_WALL_SECONDS (default 3600s) however short the caller's
# timeout is, because the inbox hook polls every 60s and composing a
# schema-valid answer takes minutes; the ScriptIT file bridge gives each
# round-trip the same 3600s. Lower it only if you accept expired requests.
# VIDZEO_AGENT_MIN_WALL_SECONDS=3600
#
# Render resilience (Nov 15, 2026, Berlin Wall scene 2): a single scene
# whose verified media no longer matches the file on disk must not hold the
# whole render. The render gate repairs it -- next ranked alternate first,
# else a clean graphic fallback re-stamped to the file's real hash -- and
# records it in the manifest's media_substitutions + graphic_allowance.
# VIDZEO_MAX_GRAPHIC_SCENES caps the graphic allowance (default 5); beyond
# it the video still renders, but the audit warns. 0 real-media scenes
# overall is still the only hard fail.
# VIDZEO_MAX_GRAPHIC_SCENES=5
#
# SIMPLIFIED PIPELINE (Nov 9, 2026) — one data pass, one script, one fix
# pass, one media pass, one monitor. No loops.
# - Research: all providers in parallel ONCE under ONE shared deadline
#   (MUSE_RESEARCH_TIMEOUT_MS, default 180000). The direct-research
#   recovery shares the same deadline — it never gets fresh time.
#   One encyclopedic synthesis call only, and only if the first pass is
#   insufficient.
# - Script: the writer makes at most MUSE_SCRIPT_MAX_DRAFTS model calls
#   (default 2: draft 1 + ONE fix pass). A draft that still fails the
#   editorial gate is returned non-publishable; the CLI exits 2 and the
#   job fails loudly instead of looping through rewrites.
# - Media: one query set per scene from the script's concrete entities,
#   one candidate fetch, one vision check. YouTube clips first, then web
#   images, then stock. Before failing a 0-real-media job the render
#   wrapper attempts ONE bounded re-plan (fresh queries, same budget,
#   MUSE_REPLAN_MIN_REMAINING_MS default 10 min left); a resume after a
#   re-plan never earns a second one.
# MUSE_SCRIPT_MAX_DRAFTS=2
# MUSE_RESEARCH_TIMEOUT_MS=180000
# MUSE_REPLAN_MIN_REMAINING_MS=600000
#
# YouTube coverage target: fraction of scenes the planner tries to fill
# with accepted YouTube clips (code default 0.6; production uses 0.30).
# MUSE_YOUTUBE_COVERAGE_TARGET=0.30
# Media strictness — FILTER-BEST (Nov 9, 2026): 0 (default) means Vidnets
# ranks candidates and keeps the best real media per scene; vision is
# advisory (an uncertain verdict never blocks delivery) and only
# named-entity scenes still require a live verdict. 1 restores the old
# hard-required flags (strict_retrieval_accuracy + visual_required) and
# hard-rejects unverified scenes. The delivery rule is separate and
# always on: the render wrapper fails a job with 0 real-media scenes.
# MUSE_MEDIA_STRICT=0
# Parallel Remotion workers — ONE video, N workers (Nov 10, 2026).
# MUSE_RENDER_CONCURRENCY=N shards ONE video's frames across N Chromium
# workers inside a single renderMedia call; Remotion stitches them into
# the one output file. It is NOT "N videos at once". The request is
# auto-degraded at render start from CPU (vCPU-1, so max 3 on this 4-vCPU
# box — 5 can never run as 5 here) and available memory (~400 MB/worker,
# 384 MB reserve; 2 workers need ~1.4 GB actually free, 3 need ~1.8 GB).
# The chosen count is logged; a degraded render still proceeds, never
# silently fails. Code default 2 is only a request; the live .env pins 1,
# the TESTED safe count — the Nov 10, 2026 benchmark degraded every
# requested level (1/2/3/5) to effective 1 worker (only ~1.1-1.2 GB
# actually free at render start), so 2/3/5 are UNPROVEN in a live render.
# Measured numbers: workflow.md §8.4 (benchmark:
# scripts/benchmark-remotion-workers.js).
# Aliases: VIDZEO_REMOTION_WORKERS / MUSE_REMOTION_WORKERS /
# VIDZEO_REMOTION_CONCURRENCY. VIDZEO_REMOTION_MAX_WORKERS caps it (8).
# MUSE_RENDER_CONCURRENCY=1
# VIDZEO_REMOTION_MEMORY_PER_WORKER_MB=400
#
# Live CMS progress log lines (Nov 10, 2026): how many recent daemon log
# lines ride along on each CMS progress POST (max 50).
# MUSE_PROGRESS_LOG_LINES=10
# Wrapper memory-pause retry delay before a Vidnets render retry (ms).
# MUSE_VIDNETS_MEMORY_RETRY_DELAY_MS=30000
# Seen-host allowlist path (Nov 10, 2026): hosts already fetched once are
# preferred for downloads, so no new per-host approval card pops.
# VIDZEO_SEEN_HOSTS_PATH=~/workspace/vidnets-runtime/seen_hosts.json
# Render memory wait (Nov 10, 2026, Vidnets server): re-check admission
# every 0.5s; extend while media workers live; fail fast 10s after the
# last media worker exits. 384 MB floor unchanged.
# VIDZEO_REMOTION_MEMORY_POLL_SECONDS=0.5
# VIDZEO_REMOTION_MEMORY_WORKER_EXTEND_SECONDS=30
# VIDZEO_REMOTION_MEMORY_NO_WORKER_GRACE_SECONDS=10
# VIDZEO_REMOTION_MEMORY_WAIT_SECONDS=120
#
# A custom writing command still wins when set. It receives the job JSON on
# stdin and prints either the narration text or {"scriptText":"..."}.
# Linux / Ubuntu example:
# MUSE_SCRIPT_COMMAND=node /opt/vidzeo/scripts/muse-script.js
# Windows example:
# MUSE_SCRIPT_COMMAND=node C:\path\to\muse-script.js
# MUSE_SCRIPT_TIMEOUT_MS=600000
# The adapter does NOT re-run ScriptIT for a short script by default: the
# script is written once, and a short result fails loudly. Opt in only to
# one bounded expansion (a second full generation) for a short first pass.
# Set to 0 to disable the single bounded expansion for a short first pass.
# MUSE_SCRIPT_EXPAND_ON_SHORT=1
# Opt-in only: bring back the old generic template synthesizer as the
# default instead of ScriptIT (labelled template-synthesizer-generic).
# MUSE_SCRIPT_ALLOW_TEMPLATE_FALLBACK=1
# OpenRouter mode for the CLI is FORBIDDEN on the production worker
# (MUSE_INFERENCE_COMPULSORY=1): the CLI refuses to run with a key set.
# It exists only for standalone/desktop use without that flag:
# SCRIPTIT_API_KEY=
# SCRIPTIT_MODEL=nvidia/nemotron-3.5-super-vl:free

# --- Video rendering ---------------------------------------------------------
# Preferred: your Muse environment's renderer. It receives JSON on stdin with
# job, scriptText, scriptPath, workDir and outputPath, and prints
# {"videoPath":"...","thumbnailPath":"..."} (thumbnailPath is optional).
# Linux / Ubuntu example:
# MUSE_RENDER_COMMAND=node /opt/vidzeo/scripts/muse-render.js
# Windows example:
# MUSE_RENDER_COMMAND=node C:\path\to\muse-render.js

# Built-in Vidnets path. The launcher must be paired locally once (see
# vidnets/AGENT_ACCESS.md). The worker starts it and services its inference
# inbox while a render runs.
# Linux: defaults to ./vidnets/VIDNETS_AGENT.sh
# Windows: defaults to .\vidnets\VIDNETS_AGENT.cmd
# MUSE_VIDNETS_COMMAND=./vidnets/VIDNETS_AGENT.sh
# MUSE_VIDNETS_ROOT=/opt/vidzeo/vidnets

# Required only when Vidnets asks for reasoning/vision. The command receives
# one request JSON on stdin and must print the exact requested answer.
# Default (shipped in this repo): the spool transport. The daemon's resident
# inbox calls it, and the resident inference worker agent answers with real
# Muse inference in seconds — see scripts/resident-inference-worker/WORKER_BRIEF.md.
# MUSE_INFERENCE_COMMAND=node ./scripts/muse-inference-spool.cjs
# HTTP alternative; accepts {"content":"..."} or plain text.
# MUSE_INFERENCE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:7777/answer
# The resident worker is the only real transport shipped here. Never point
# this at a stub that returns fixed scores — the deleted
# muse-inference-engine.cjs did exactly that (always approved everything).

# Built-in Vidnets stock rescue providers.
# PEXELS_API_KEY=
# PIXABAY_API_KEY=
# (MUSE_RENDER_CONCURRENCY and MUSE_YOUTUBE_COVERAGE_TARGET live in the
# simplified-pipeline block above — one definition each, no duplicates.)
# Parallel fragments per streaming download, from 1 to 8. Measure host
# throughput and throttling before raising this alongside download workers.
# VIDZEO_YT_FRAGMENT_CONCURRENCY=2

# --- Thumbnail ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional custom thumbnail command. Receives JSON on stdin with job,
# scriptText, videoPath and workDir; prints {"thumbnailPath":"..."}.
# MUSE_THUMBNAIL_COMMAND=node /opt/vidzeo/scripts/muse-thumbnail.js
# Frame extraction fallback position in seconds; otherwise a safe early frame.
# MUSE_THUMBNAIL_AT_SECONDS=

# --- Media & Python tools ----------------------------------------------------
# FFprobe/FFmpeg are required for local master and thumbnail checks.
# On Linux/Ubuntu: usually discovered automatically at /usr/bin/ffmpeg or via PATH.
# FFPROBE_PATH=ffprobe
# FFMPEG_PATH=ffmpeg
# MUSE_FFMPEG_DIR=/usr/bin
# Python 3 path (Linux/Ubuntu: python3, Windows: python)
# VIDNETS_PYTHON=python3
#
# Temp dirs: /tmp on this VM is a 512 MB tmpfs and has filled up before, so
# Vidnets/Remotion scratch goes to disk under VIDZEO_TEMP_DIR (TMPDIR is the
# same dir for Node/Python tempfile users). The render wrapper refuses to
# start when runtime temp has < 512 MB or /tmp has < 128 MB free, and
# start-daemon.sh prunes known scratch older than 7 days at daemon start
# (never temp/jobs, media, caches, or manifests).
# TMPDIR=/home/hatch/workspace/vidnets-runtime/temp
# VIDZEO_TEMP_DIR=/home/hatch/workspace/vidnets-runtime/temp

# --- CMS outage resilience (Nov 9, 2026) ------------------------------------
# A transient CMS error (HTTP 502/503/530, tunnel down, transport failure)
# never kills the daemon: resumeActiveJob retries the saved-job probe with
# exponential backoff (MUSE_CMS_RESUME_ATTEMPTS, default 12) and then
# defers the resume — the daemon keeps polling and re-attempts it, so it
# survives hours of CMS outage and resumes cleanly when the CMS returns.
# The queue poll backs off the same way (base MUSE_POLL_MS, cap
# MUSE_CMS_RETRY_MAX_MS). Only 401 (revoked credential) still exits.
# MUSE_CMS_RESUME_ATTEMPTS=12
# MUSE_CMS_RETRY_BASE_MS=2000
# MUSE_CMS_RETRY_MAX_MS=60000

# --- Bounds ------------------------------------------------------------------
# MUSE_RENDER_TIMEOUT_MS=21600000
# MUSE_UPLOAD_TIMEOUT_MS=1800000
# MUSE_POLL_MS=3000
# MUSE_HEARTBEAT_MS=20000
# MUSE_PROGRESS_KEEPALIVE_MS=90000
